  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q76-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "76",
    "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}+\\dfrac{1}{\\log_b(ab)}=1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}+\\dfrac{1}{\\log_b(ab)}=1$.\n\nChange both logarithms to a common base and apply the product law.",
      "worked": "For $a,b>0$, $a\\ne1$, $b\\ne1$ and $ab\\ne1$, change both logarithms to base $e$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(ab)}&=\\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln(ab)}+\\frac{\\ln b}{\\ln(ab)}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(ab)}&=\\frac{\\ln a+\\ln b}{\\ln(ab)}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(ab)}&=\\frac{\\ln(ab)}{\\ln(ab)}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(ab)}&=1.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
